      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Basements, plant rooms, rural sites, tunnels and steel-framed buildings all lose signal. You can keep inspecting. Here is what happens to your messages, and what Quickler does with them when signal comes back.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>WhatsApp holds the messages you send without signal and delivers them when signal returns. Quickler does not need a connection on your phone to sync.</li><li>Messages that reach Quickler close together, each within 90 seconds of the one before, are treated as one walk.</li><li>A burst can land out of order. A text uploads faster than a voice note or a photo. Quickler still files them to the right walk.</li><li>A done sent without signal still counts, once the rest of the burst has arrived.</li><li>If no message reaches Quickler for 12 hours, the walk is filed as it stands.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The places",
      "heading": "Where signal drops",
      "html": "<p>Signal drops in the same places on most sites: basements and car parks below ground, plant rooms and lift shafts, rural sites and farm buildings, tunnels and culverts, and steel-framed or heavily screened buildings. A phone with no signal can still take photos and record voice notes. It cannot send them.</p><p>Quickler runs in WhatsApp or Telegram, as they already work on your phone. There is no separate app that has to sync, so there is nothing extra to set up for a dead spot.</p>"
    },
