      "heading": "What Quickler does with a late or out-of-order burst",
      "html": "<p><strong>Burst.</strong> Messages from one phone that arrive close together, each within 90 seconds of the one before, are treated as one walk, in whatever order they land. A message from before the last filing never joins a new burst, so an old done cannot close a new walk.</p><p><strong>Out of order.</strong> A text uploads faster than media, so a done can land before the voice note that starts the Check. When a Check starts, it takes in its whole burst: the voice note, the photos and the early done are all that walk. Quickler uses when each message reached it, not when you wrote it.</p><p><strong>Voice notes.</strong> A voice note still being written out is waited for, so the Check starts on what you said. A spoken done counts as well as a typed one.</p><p><strong>Photos.</strong> Photos from a burst stay with the walk they belong to. They are sorted onto questions after the walk, using the answers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The limit",
      "heading": "When 12 hours pass",
      "html": "<p>If no message reaches Quickler for 12 hours, a started walk is filed as it stands. It is marked as filed on a time-out, and questions you did not mention are not marked as passed. You are told it was filed after 12 hours with no messages, and that you can send anything else to the same chat.</p><p>After a filing, anything you send within 12 hours is judged as an addition to the Record just filed or the start of a new walk. If it is an addition, the Record is updated rather than a second one created.</p><p>Quickler keeps every message that reaches it. What it cannot do is receive a message that never leaves your phone, so if you are stuck without signal for 12 hours, the walk is filed with what had arrived.</p>"
    },
